<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry the Fowler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 876–<a href="/years/936">936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Henry I of Germany, Henry the Fowler of Saxony · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry the Fowler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a>, was elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> in 919, the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a>. He won over the dukes of Swabia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, regained Lotharingia, campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> from the Hevelli, and defeated the Hungarians at Riade in 933. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> became emperor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler.jpg" alt="Henry the Fowler" width="315" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siegel_Heinrich_I_Posse.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was born in Memleben, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, into the Liudolfing line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> dukes, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-the-illustrious">Otto the Illustrious</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a>. His mother, Hedwiga, was probably a great-granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> through her mother. In 906 he married Hatheburg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a>, a former nun; the church annulled the marriage in 909. In the same year he married Matilda, daughter of Dietrich of Ringelheim, who was later canonised. He succeeded his father as duke in 912 and rebelled against King Conrad I over lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> before they reconciled in 915.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Conrad I died on 23 December 918 after recommending Henry as king, since only he could hold the kingdom together against revolts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> raids. At the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritzlar">Fritzlar</a> in 919 Franconian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> nobles elected him, the first non-Frank on the throne. Archbishop Heriger of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> offered to anoint him, but he refused, the only king of his time to do so, allegedly because he wished to be king by acclamation of the people and not by the church's. He then had to subdue the other dukes. Arnulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, who had crowned himself king of Bavaria in 919, submitted only in 921, keeping substantial autonomy.</p><p>Henry did not seek a centralised monarchy and ruled through autonomous stem duchies. In 925, after Duke Gilbert of Lotharingia rebelled, he invaded and besieged Gilbert at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulpich">Zülpich</a>, then in 928 married his daughter Gerberga to Gilbert, bringing Lotharingia back as the fifth stem duchy. He agreed a ten-year truce with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> in 924 in return for annual tribute, and used it to build fortified towns and train a cavalry force. In 932 he refused the tribute, and in 933 he crushed the Magyars at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-riade">Battle of Riade</a>.</p><p>During the truce he marched on the Hevelli in the winter of 928 and seized their capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. In 929 he forced Duke Wenceslaus I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> to resume tribute, and in 934 he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Henry planned a journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to be crowned emperor, but died on 2 July 936 and was buried at Quedlinburg Abbey, founded by Matilda. He had prevented a collapse of royal power such as occurred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> and left a stronger kingdom to his son Otto I, the first of the Ottonian rulers who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> from 919 until 1024. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a> made him a character in his opera Lohengrin in 1850.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry the Fowler” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Henry the Fowler</b>, as king, prepared for the Magyar threat by negotiating a nine-year truce in 926, building fortifications and training cavalry, and defeated the Magyars at the Battle of Riade in 933.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duchy of Saxony, ruled by the Liudolfing family, produced the kings of East Francia from 919, beginning with <b>Henry the Fowler</b>, and his son Otto I was crowned emperor in 962.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>When Louis the Child, the last Carolingian ruler of East Francia, died in 911, the dukes and magnates elected Conrad, Duke of Franconia, as their king, and in 919 chose the Saxon duke <b>Henry the Fowler</b>.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Henry the Fowler</b> of East Francia routed a Magyar army near the river Unstrut in northern Thuringia, after refusing to pay further tribute.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-15">15 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At Fritzlar, the nobles of Franconia and Saxony elected <b>Henry the Fowler</b>, Duke of Saxony, king of East Francia, the first Saxon to take the throne.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-24">24 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles the Simple, king of West Francia, and <b>Henry the Fowler</b>, king of East Francia, signed a &quot;pact of friendship&quot; aboard a ship in the middle of the Rhine near Bonn.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-7">7 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Memleben, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Memleben, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.27,11.49&amp;year=915" aria-label="Memleben on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Henry the Fowler"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5611 5691h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5611 5691h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.27,11.49&amp;year=915">What was here in 915 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
